1
00:00:00,740 --> 00:00:05,399
This is the second video in the series
about bootloaders and device trees.

2
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:10,338
So we're going to introduce some different
bootloader options, U-Boot and UEFI.

3
00:00:10,338 --> 00:00:14,373
We're going to understand some basic
device tree modifications for Raspberry,

4
00:00:14,373 --> 00:00:15,404
Pi and Beaglebone.

5
00:00:15,404 --> 00:00:19,246
And go through how the device tree works
with those particular components that you

6
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:21,095
might be using for your final project.

7
00:00:22,912 --> 00:00:27,392
So, UEFI is the Universal
Extensible Firmware Interface.

8
00:00:27,392 --> 00:00:32,769
So this is something that's
common on x86/x86_64 designs.

9
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:34,648
It's also available on some designs.

10
00:00:34,648 --> 00:00:39,469
And if you're familiar with the old
school bios that used to be a part or

11
00:00:39,469 --> 00:00:44,882
a standard part of the boot process for
PCs, UEFI is the replacement for that.

12
00:00:44,882 --> 00:00:49,624
So, it's similar to the content we talked
about in the previous video, it will use

13
00:00:49,624 --> 00:00:54,262
the same three phase bootloader type of
description that we talked about there.

14
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:58,460
So this might be one option
that you have as a bootloader,

15
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:04,773
especially if you're using x86 or
x86_64 as your embedded system platform.

16
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:11,343
The book also talks about several
different other bootloader options in

17
00:01:11,343 --> 00:01:15,293
Mastering Embedded Linux Programming,
chapter three.

18
00:01:15,293 --> 00:01:21,229
And here's a list that comes out of the
book in terms of different bootloaders.

19
00:01:21,229 --> 00:01:24,980
The ones I've used personally
are U-Boot and GRUB 2.

20
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,006
I'm not familiar with the rest of these.

21
00:01:27,006 --> 00:01:30,583
And so, the book talks about some
of the ways that you would pick

22
00:01:30,583 --> 00:01:35,240
a bootloader in the case that you have
different bootloaders to pick from.

23
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:39,441
So you would figure out, first of all,
does it support your platform?

24
00:01:39,441 --> 00:01:40,982
Is it provided with your BSP?

25
00:01:40,982 --> 00:01:45,359
This is a big one, because most likely
whoever develops your hardware is going to

26
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:46,833
have a reference design.

27
00:01:46,833 --> 00:01:49,356
And if you can start with
whatever they're using,

28
00:01:49,356 --> 00:01:51,298
that's often the best place to start.

29
00:01:51,298 --> 00:01:53,605
Is source code available?

30
00:01:53,605 --> 00:01:55,816
We've talked about that
a lot in this class.

31
00:01:55,816 --> 00:01:59,232
That is a nice thing to have
the availability to modify the source,

32
00:01:59,232 --> 00:02:00,030
if you need to.

33
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:02,561
Or to try to figure out the problem,
if there is a problem.

34
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:06,301
And then especially,
if you don't have source available,

35
00:02:06,301 --> 00:02:08,440
are you able to get support if needed?

36
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:13,665
So again, a reason why you might
consider open source solution,

37
00:02:13,665 --> 00:02:20,195
and GRUB and U-Boot would both be
open source options as well as UEFI.

38
00:02:20,195 --> 00:02:26,100
So talking a little bit more about U-Boot,
U-Boots, a pretty popular ARM bootloader.

39
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:31,030
It started as an open source bootloader
for PowerPC, and then was ported to ARM.

40
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:35,140
So, it's pretty common to see
that used on ARM platforms today.

41
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:39,968
And if you take a look at how U-Boot build
works, it's again, using that kconfig,

42
00:02:39,968 --> 00:02:44,388
defconfig type of setup that we've been
familiar with in multiple different

43
00:02:44,388 --> 00:02:46,180
parts of this course already.

44
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:50,742
So, it'll feel pretty similar to you with
respect to building U-Boot as it would be

45
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:53,440
with working with the Linux kernel.

46
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:58,596
It builds uboot.bin an image file
that's suitable for running directly on

47
00:02:58,596 --> 00:03:04,407
the device or programming into flash, and
then loading from flash to our own device.

48
00:03:04,407 --> 00:03:09,767
And then depending on the architecture,
it may also build that secondary program

49
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:15,062
loader or the first thing that we run out
of a RAM, basically, also known as MLO.

50
00:03:15,062 --> 00:03:19,992
Or so the really early stage bootloader
is supported on several devices as well.

51
00:03:22,334 --> 00:03:27,151
Usually to install U-Boot, you'll need
hardware specific instructions, and

52
00:03:27,151 --> 00:03:30,520
often, you'll need something
like a JTAG programmer.

53
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:34,227
So, this is probably the initial installs,
probably going to

54
00:03:34,227 --> 00:03:39,190
happen during a factory programming step,
an initial bring up of the hardware.

55
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:44,385
It's also possible in some cases or some
hardware architectures to load directly

56
00:03:44,385 --> 00:03:49,141
into RAM with JTAG, and then run out
of RAM and bootstrap right into flash.

57
00:03:49,141 --> 00:03:52,969
So, you'll have to read the details of
a specific device to figure out how

58
00:03:52,969 --> 00:03:54,940
you can get U-Boot installed.

59
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:59,786
But there are a couple different ways that
you most likely be able to install it.

60
00:03:59,786 --> 00:04:02,729
Once you have it installed and
you're using it for

61
00:04:02,729 --> 00:04:05,060
kind of troubleshooting and bring up.

62
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:09,440
There's a serial terminal you can
use to access over a serial port.

63
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:13,944
And so, again, that access will vary
depending on which device you're using.

64
00:04:13,944 --> 00:04:17,340
But most devices are going to
have some kind of capability for

65
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,107
a serial port input to
interact with U-Boot.

66
00:04:20,107 --> 00:04:24,802
And you'll be able to interact similar
to the way you do from the Linux-Colonel

67
00:04:24,802 --> 00:04:25,450
end type.

68
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:27,669
Except that the commands
will be more limited,

69
00:04:27,669 --> 00:04:30,780
you won't be using anything like
Bash scripting, for instance.

70
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:34,090
There will just be a specific built-in
set of commands that U-Boot supports.

71
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:36,626
You can type help to
see all those commands.

72
00:04:36,626 --> 00:04:41,738
And U-Boot ultimately, uses environment
variables to either pass information or

73
00:04:41,738 --> 00:04:46,355
to script a series of actions and commands
in order to get the boot complete.

74
00:04:46,355 --> 00:04:50,657
And those script commands are initially
defined in header files for the platform,

75
00:04:50,657 --> 00:04:54,667
that are part of what you compile when
you compile U-Boot for your platform.

76
00:04:56,231 --> 00:04:59,261
So, some of the basics of how
to use U-Boot from the terminal.

77
00:04:59,261 --> 00:05:03,036
You can set environment variables
with the setenv command.

78
00:05:03,036 --> 00:05:06,560
And you can save environment variables,
then if you want to save them for

79
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:09,540
persistent memory with
the saveenv command.

80
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:15,054
And then it also has commands that would
let you load images into RAM from flash or

81
00:05:15,054 --> 00:05:15,940
tftp.

82
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:18,880
It has commands to boot
a kernel image from RAM.

83
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:22,927
So an example of how you use this,
you'd say bootm, and

84
00:05:22,927 --> 00:05:27,090
you'd give it the address of
the kernel location in RAM.

85
00:05:27,090 --> 00:05:31,860
The address of the RAM disk or
where the ramdisk is stored in RAM and

86
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,609
the address of the dtb.

87
00:05:33,609 --> 00:05:38,096
So, it would obviously assume that you've
been able to load these all into memory

88
00:05:38,096 --> 00:05:39,538
from some previous step.

89
00:05:39,538 --> 00:05:44,256
And that's where these commands to load
images from flash or tftp would be useful.

90
00:05:44,256 --> 00:05:45,306
In the tftp case,

91
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:50,075
what you're saying is especially from
a development perspective, you've got

92
00:05:50,075 --> 00:05:54,512
a local server on your network that is
able to serve up these binary images.

93
00:05:54,512 --> 00:05:58,321
So that you don't have to get
the flash interface working, or

94
00:05:58,321 --> 00:06:02,506
you don't have to reprogram it every
time you want to try a new image.

95
00:06:03,510 --> 00:06:07,951
Then the boot command is a variable that
is run at power up that you can define

96
00:06:07,951 --> 00:06:10,843
with a series of commands
that will ultimately,

97
00:06:10,843 --> 00:06:15,590
be used to boot up your device, get to
the point of running the Linux-Colonel.

98
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:22,146
So talking through some specific
examples about how bootloader setup and

99
00:06:22,146 --> 00:06:25,985
device tree works for some of
the hardware components that will use or

100
00:06:25,985 --> 00:06:30,040
you may be using this semester
in your final projects.

101
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:34,337
Any time you're talking about bootloader
and device tree, in order to get past much

102
00:06:34,337 --> 00:06:38,527
more than generalities, you usually have
to pick a specific hardware platform.

103
00:06:38,527 --> 00:06:41,470
Because this is going to
be hardware specific.

104
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:45,473
So, we'll go through a couple of
specific hardware examples for

105
00:06:45,473 --> 00:06:47,940
Raspberry Pi and BeagleBone Black.

106
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:52,640
Since those are some parts that you might
want to use with your final projects.

107
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:57,639
So the Raspberry Pi has something
that looks sort of like a bios type

108
00:06:57,639 --> 00:07:02,207
of configuration that's driven
by this config.txt file.

109
00:07:02,207 --> 00:07:07,055
And it's up to you to specify any
customization that you want to

110
00:07:07,055 --> 00:07:08,435
the device tree.

111
00:07:08,435 --> 00:07:12,421
For instance,
to use things like I2C, or SPI, or

112
00:07:12,421 --> 00:07:18,661
any hardware components that would be off
by default in the Raspberry Pi build.

113
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:21,469
And there are some links here too,

114
00:07:21,469 --> 00:07:23,802
some pages that talk about
how you can configure it.

115
00:07:23,802 --> 00:07:27,489
It's a pretty simple text based format
that's going to customize the device

116
00:07:27,489 --> 00:07:28,201
tree for you.

117
00:07:29,818 --> 00:07:34,612
So again, that configuration file is the
equivalent of the bios that you'd find on

118
00:07:34,612 --> 00:07:39,240
a conventional PC or the equivalent
of what's been replaced by UEFI.

119
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:43,280
And these are stored in this text file,
config.txt.

120
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:46,510
And you can make the changes
in that config.txt to change

121
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:48,140
the device configuration.

122
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:53,154
So for instance, if you wanted to change
your device tree to turn on audio,

123
00:07:53,154 --> 00:07:56,347
use I2C,
with a specific bit rate turn on SPI.

124
00:07:56,347 --> 00:08:00,207
That's an example, things you
could do in that config.txt file,

125
00:08:00,207 --> 00:08:04,284
that's going to customize the content
of the device tree binary for you.

126
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:10,194
And there's a page we have
in our hardware hints for

127
00:08:10,194 --> 00:08:16,740
the class that talk about how you could
change config.txt on a running system.

128
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:21,376
Using these mount commands to first
create a location where you can mount

129
00:08:21,376 --> 00:08:22,445
the partition.

130
00:08:22,445 --> 00:08:26,282
And then mount this
mmcblk0p1 to that location.

131
00:08:26,282 --> 00:08:29,178
And change whatever you
want to in the config.txt.

132
00:08:29,178 --> 00:08:33,788
So, this hardware hints page
talks about how to turn SPI on

133
00:08:33,788 --> 00:08:37,187
by changing this dtparam in config.txt.

134
00:08:38,743 --> 00:08:41,935
So, you might make a change to
the device tree, and then wonder how

135
00:08:41,935 --> 00:08:45,993
can you verify that the change is actually
made successfully on the running system.

136
00:08:45,993 --> 00:08:49,774
Or this might be a good way to
troubleshoot, if you believe you've made

137
00:08:49,774 --> 00:08:54,201
changes, but you aren't seeing something
available that you expect to be there.

138
00:08:54,201 --> 00:08:57,515
So this talks about
the example of figuring

139
00:08:57,515 --> 00:09:00,375
out whether you have a spy turned on.

140
00:09:00,375 --> 00:09:06,166
And we talked about the device
tree compiler as going from a text

141
00:09:06,166 --> 00:09:12,407
file to a binary file or a flattened
device tree or device tree blob.

142
00:09:12,407 --> 00:09:15,368
But you can also go backwards
with the device tree compiler,

143
00:09:15,368 --> 00:09:18,926
you can take the output and turn that
back into a human-readable input.

144
00:09:18,926 --> 00:09:20,358
So, this is often useful for

145
00:09:20,358 --> 00:09:24,189
figuring out what device tree content
is currently running on your device.

146
00:09:24,189 --> 00:09:30,841
So, if I take the dtc command and
do a -I fs -O dts, what that saying?

147
00:09:30,841 --> 00:09:34,524
Is that the input is the sys
fs file system tree, and

148
00:09:34,524 --> 00:09:39,506
the output is device tree source,
so the human-readable content.

149
00:09:39,506 --> 00:09:41,939
And then if you look at that content,

150
00:09:41,939 --> 00:09:47,206
you can find the specific spy instance at
a specific location in the memory map.

151
00:09:47,206 --> 00:09:53,005
And you can see that the change should
be that it goes from disabled to okay,

152
00:09:53,005 --> 00:09:58,183
once you've configured it
properly in your config.txt.

153
00:09:58,183 --> 00:10:01,286
So switching gears a bit and
going to Beaglebone.

154
00:10:01,286 --> 00:10:06,441
There are some links here on this page
that talked about what a student has done

155
00:10:06,441 --> 00:10:11,540
in the past to make some configurations
to enable that PARCH on Beaglebone.

156
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:17,420
And so, this is all shared as actually
as a part of a extra credit step for

157
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:22,126
their final project, so
students can use it currently.

158
00:10:22,126 --> 00:10:25,213
But if you need to go
through a similar process,

159
00:10:25,213 --> 00:10:30,078
there's some links here that show you
the process the student went through

160
00:10:30,078 --> 00:10:34,339
in order to create the device
tree modification for Beaglebone.

161
00:10:34,339 --> 00:10:38,647
And so again, a lot of this gets into
the specific details for the platform, but

162
00:10:38,647 --> 00:10:43,469
it's ultimately, about making a change to
the device tree, include file, this dtsi,

163
00:10:43,469 --> 00:10:45,367
device tree source include file.

164
00:10:45,367 --> 00:10:48,187
To include the specification for

165
00:10:48,187 --> 00:10:53,029
enabling specific pins for
this uart1 functionality.

166
00:10:53,029 --> 00:10:58,180
And so, adding the definition
of the pin control pins.

167
00:10:58,180 --> 00:11:01,980
And then specifying that
definition here in the uart1, and

168
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:05,715
specifically saying that we
wanted to enable uart1 here.

169
00:11:07,343 --> 00:11:11,133
And so, what does this look like in
order to actually make the change,

170
00:11:11,133 --> 00:11:14,875
or how do you figure out that this is
where you need to make the change?

171
00:11:14,875 --> 00:11:19,829
You can start by looking at the REPO_URL
that's used by the build road configure to

172
00:11:19,829 --> 00:11:23,933
see where the source codes coming
from before they get kernel, or for

173
00:11:23,933 --> 00:11:25,084
the Linux kernel.

174
00:11:25,084 --> 00:11:29,982
And therefore, where the device tree
include files are going to be built from.

175
00:11:29,982 --> 00:11:33,745
And then once you look
at the content of that,

176
00:11:33,745 --> 00:11:39,690
you can see references to the dtsi,
the device resource include file,

177
00:11:39,690 --> 00:11:44,172
and the place where we needed
to add patches for uart.

178
00:11:44,172 --> 00:11:48,544
And within that you can see
a reference to the uart1

179
00:11:48,544 --> 00:11:53,842
definition that we've added in
that previous include file.

180
00:11:53,842 --> 00:11:58,106
And in order to understand what the
different values are you can enter here in

181
00:11:58,106 --> 00:12:02,903
this field for the device tree, there's
a documentation directory under the kernel

182
00:12:02,903 --> 00:12:06,960
source that talks about the different
properties you can specify.

183
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:10,940
And so, this is where you can
specify the list of names from

184
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:13,867
the documentation specific to this part,

185
00:12:13,867 --> 00:12:19,740
that can reference the uart definition
that we just declared in the dtsi file.

186
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:24,951
So again, you kind of have to read this
with the idea of digging into how it works

187
00:12:24,951 --> 00:12:30,110
on a specific platform, because all
device trees are somewhat different.

188
00:12:30,110 --> 00:12:32,589
But here's some high level
steps you can go through,

189
00:12:32,589 --> 00:12:36,003
if you find yourself in a place that you
need to make device tree changes for

190
00:12:36,003 --> 00:12:38,060
